"""Original glossary, prompt library and FAQ content for GDTai."""

GLOSSARY = [
    ("Agent", "An AI system that plans and takes multiple actions (calling tools, browsing, writing files) to reach a goal, instead of answering a single prompt."),
    ("Alignment", "Research and techniques that make AI systems behave according to human intentions and values."),
    ("API", "Application Programming Interface — the way software sends requests to an AI model and receives results, usually billed per token."),
    ("Attention", "The mechanism inside transformers that lets a model weigh which earlier words matter most when producing the next one."),
    ("Benchmark", "A standard test set used to compare models, such as coding, maths or reasoning suites. Useful, but never the full picture."),
    ("Chain of thought", "Having a model work through intermediate reasoning steps before giving a final answer, which often improves accuracy on hard problems."),
    ("Chatbot", "A conversational interface to an AI model. Modern chatbots are powered by large language models."),
    ("Context window", "The maximum amount of text (measured in tokens) a model can consider at once, including your prompt, files and its reply."),
    ("Diffusion model", "A generative model that creates images, audio or video by gradually removing noise from random data."),
    ("Distillation", "Training a smaller, cheaper model to imitate a larger one so it keeps much of the quality at lower cost."),
    ("Embedding", "A list of numbers representing the meaning of text or images, used for semantic search, clustering and recommendations."),
    ("Evals", "Systematic tests you write for your own AI feature to measure quality, regressions and failure modes."),
    ("Few-shot prompting", "Including a handful of worked examples in a prompt so the model copies the pattern and format you want."),
    ("Fine-tuning", "Further training a pre-trained model on your own examples to specialise its style or task performance."),
    ("Foundation model", "A large model trained on broad data that can be adapted to many downstream tasks."),
    ("Function calling", "A model returning a structured request to run a specific tool or function, letting apps connect AI to real systems."),
    ("Generative AI", "AI that produces new content — text, images, audio, video or code — rather than only classifying existing data."),
    ("GPU", "Graphics Processing Unit — the parallel chips used to train and run most modern AI models."),
    ("Grounding", "Tying a model's answer to trusted sources such as documents or search results to reduce made-up information."),
    ("Guardrails", "Rules, filters and checks placed around an AI system to keep its outputs safe, on-topic and compliant."),
    ("Hallucination", "When a model states something false with confidence. Mitigated with grounding, citations and verification."),
    ("Inference", "Running a trained model to get outputs. Inference cost and speed matter most for live products."),
    ("Jailbreak", "An attempt to trick a model into ignoring its safety rules. Responsible builders test against these."),
    ("Knowledge cutoff", "The date after which a model has no training data, so it may not know recent events without search."),
    ("Large language model (LLM)", "A neural network trained on huge amounts of text to predict and generate language."),
    ("Latency", "How long a model takes to respond. Important for chat, voice and real-time applications."),
    ("LoRA", "Low-Rank Adaptation — an efficient fine-tuning method that trains small add-on weights instead of the whole model."),
    ("MCP", "Model Context Protocol — an open standard for connecting AI assistants to tools and data sources in a consistent way."),
    ("Mixture of experts (MoE)", "A model design where only some specialised sub-networks activate per token, improving efficiency at scale."),
    ("Multimodal", "Able to understand or generate more than one type of data, such as text plus images, audio or video."),
    ("Neural network", "Layers of connected mathematical units whose weights are learned from data; the basis of deep learning."),
    ("Open weights", "Models whose trained parameters are published so anyone can download, run and adapt them."),
    ("Overfitting", "When a model memorises its training examples and performs poorly on new data."),
    ("Parameters", "The learned numeric weights inside a model. More parameters often means more capability and higher cost."),
    ("Prompt", "The instructions and context you give a model. Clear goals, constraints and examples produce better results."),
    ("Prompt injection", "Hidden or malicious instructions in content an AI reads that try to hijack its behaviour."),
    ("Quantization", "Storing model weights at lower precision so it runs faster and uses less memory, with small quality trade-offs."),
    ("RAG", "Retrieval-Augmented Generation — fetching relevant documents first and giving them to the model so answers are grounded."),
    ("Reasoning model", "A model trained to spend extra compute thinking through problems before answering, improving maths, code and logic."),
    ("Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF)", "Training a model using human preference ratings so its responses become more helpful and safe."),
    ("Semantic search", "Search by meaning using embeddings, so a query finds relevant content even without matching keywords."),
    ("System prompt", "Top-level instructions that set an assistant's role, rules and tone for an entire conversation."),
    ("Temperature", "A setting controlling randomness. Low values give focused, repeatable output; high values give more variety."),
    ("Text-to-speech (TTS)", "Converting written text into spoken audio with synthetic voices."),
    ("Token", "A chunk of text (roughly ¾ of an English word) that models read and write. Pricing and limits are counted in tokens."),
    ("Tool use", "A model's ability to call external tools — search, calculators, code execution, APIs — to complete tasks."),
    ("Training data", "The examples a model learns from. Its quality, licensing and diversity shape model behaviour."),
    ("Transformer", "The neural network architecture behind modern LLMs, built around attention."),
    ("Vector database", "A database optimised to store embeddings and find the most similar items quickly; core to RAG."),
    ("Vibe coding", "Building software mainly by describing what you want to an AI and iterating on its output."),
    ("Zero-shot", "Asking a model to do a task with no examples, relying on its general training."),
]

